Since $h(x) = f^{-1}(x)$, we have $f(h(x)) = x$ for all $x$ in the domain of $h$. 
Given $h(2) = 10$, we have $f(10) = 2$.
Given $h(10) = 1$, we have $f(1) = 10$.
Given $h(1) = 2$, we have $f(2) = 1$.
We want to find $f(f(10))$. Since $f(10) = 2$, we have
$f(f(10)) = f(2) = 1$.

Thus, $f(f(10)) = 1$.

