\begin{align*}
\label{eq:1}
\text{Find the matrix that corresponds to projection onto the } y\text{-axis.}
\end{align*}
Let $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}$ be a vector in $\mathbb{R}^2$. The projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $y$-axis is given by
\[ \text{proj}_y \mathbf{v} = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{i}}{\|\mathbf{i}\|^2} \mathbf{i} = \frac{\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\left\| \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \right\|^2} \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \frac{y}{1} \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ y \end{pmatrix}. \]
The matrix corresponding to projection onto the $y$-axis is
\[ \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}. \]
Let $\mathbf{x}$ be an arbitrary vector in $\mathbb{R}^2$. Then
\[ \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0x + 0y \\ 0x + 1y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \text{proj}_y \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}. \]

Therefore, the required matrix is
\[ \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}} \]
\boxed{<beginpmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix>}

