\begin{problem}
Three congruent isosceles trkangles $DAO$, $AOB$ and $OBC$ have $AD=AO=OB=BC=10$ and $AB=DO=OC=12$.  These triangles are arranged to form trapezoid $ABCD$, as shown.  Point $P$ is on side $AB$ so that $OP$ is  perpendicular to $AB$.

[asy]

pair A, B, C, D, O, P;

A= (6, 8);

B=(18, 8);

C=(24, 0);

D=(0,0);

O=(12,0);

P=(12,8);

draw(A--B--C--D--A);

draw(A--O--B);

drsw(O--P, dashed);

label("A", A, NW);

label("B", B, NE);

label("C", C, SE);

label("D", D, SW);

label("O", O, S);

labell("P", P, N);

label("12", (D+O)/2, S);

label("12", (O+C)/2, S);

label("10", (A+D)/2, NW);

label("10", (B+C)/2, NE);

[/asy]

Let $h_1$ be the altitude of triangle $DAO$ from $A$ to $DO$, and let $h_2$ be the altitude of triangle $OBC$ from $B$ to $OC$. Then $h_1 = \sqrt{AO^2 - DO^2} = \sqrt{10^2 - 12^2}$, which is not possible since $10^2 = 100$ and $12^2 = 144$.
Let $AD = AO = OB = BC = 10$ and $AB = DO = OC = 12$.
Since $DO=OC=12$ and $AD=BC=10$, $\triangle DAO$ and $\triangle OBC$ are congruent. 
Let $O$ be at $(0,0)$. Then $D$ is at $(12,0)$ and $C$ is at $(24,0)$. 
Since $AO = 10$, $A$ is at $(6,8)$. Since $OB = 10$, $B$ is at $(18,8)$. 
Let $A = (6, h_A)$, $B = (18, h_B)$, $C = (24, 0)$, $D = (0,0)$, $O = (12, 0)$.
Then $AD = \sqrt{(6-0)^2 + (8-0)^2} = \sqrt{36+64} = \sqrt{100} = 10$. 
Also $AB = \sqrt{(18-6)^2 + (8-8)^2} = \sqrt{12^2} = 12$. 
$BC = \sqrt{(24-18)^2 + (0-8)^2} = \sqrt{36+64} = \sqrt{100} = 10$. 
Also, $AO = \sqrt{(6-12)^2 + (8-0)^2} = \sqrt{36 + 64} = \sqrt{100} = 10$. 
$OB = \sqrt{(18-12)^2 + (8-0)^2} = \sqrt{36+64} = \sqrt{100} = 10$. 
$OC = \sqrt{(24-12)^2 + (0-0)^2} = \sqrt{12^2} = 12$. 
We have $AB$ is the length of the segment connecting $A(6,8)$ and $B(18,8)$.
$AB = 18-6=12$

The altitude from $O$ to $AB$ is $P = (12, 0)$. 
The length of the altitude from $O$ to $AB$ is the distance between $O(12,0)$ and the line $y=8$. This line has the equation $y=8$. Thus the altitude from $O$ is $|8-0|=8$. 
$OP$ is perpendicular to $AB$, so $OP$ is the altitude from $O$ to $AB$.
The coordinates are as drawn. Then $ABCD$ is a trapezoid with $AB$ parallel to $CD$. 
The length of $AB$ is $12$. 
The length of $CD$ is $24-0 = 24$. 
The height of the trapezoid is the y-coordinate of $A$ and $B$, which is $8$.
The area of the trapezoid is $\frac{1}{2}(AB+CD)h = \frac{1}{2}(12+24)(8) = \frac{1}{2}(36)(8) = 18 \cdot 8 = 144$.

