The original cube has dimensions $2 \text{ cm} \times 2 \text{ cm} \times 2 \text{ cm}$. The volume of the original cube is $V = 2 \times 2 \times 2 = 8 \text{ cm}^3$.
The volume is increased by 19 cubic centimeters, so the new volume is $V_{new} = 8 + 19 = 27 \text{ cm}^3$.
Let $s$ be the length of one edge of the new cube. The volume of the new cube is $V_{new} = s^3$. Thus, $s^3 = 27$.
Taking the cube root of both sides, we get $s = \sqrt[3]{27} = 3 \text{ cm}$.

\boxed{3 \text{ cm}}
